    <name>
      <text>Differentiate a rational trig function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the derivative of the following function:</p>
<p>\(\quad \displaystyle P(t)={@pt@}\)</p>
<p>\(\quad \displaystyle P'(t)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To differentiate the given function \(\displaystyle P(t) = {@pt@}\), we can use the quotient rule for differentiation.</p>
<p> The quotient rule states that if you have a function of the form \( \displaystyle \frac{u(t)}{v(t)}\), then its derivative is given by:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{u(t)}{v(t)}\right) = \frac{u'(t)v(t) - u(t)v'(t)}{\big[v(t)\big]^2}\)</p>
<p>In this case, \(\displaystyle u(t) ={@ut@}\) and \(\displaystyle v(t) = {@vt@}\). We need to find the derivatives of both \(u(t)\) and \(v(t)\) with respect to \(t\)</p>
<p> \(\displaystyle u(t) ={@ut@}\,  \implies  \, u'(t)={@du@}\) and </p>
<p> \(\displaystyle v(t) ={@vt@}\,  \implies  \, v'(t)={@dv@}\)</p>
<p>Now, we apply the quotient rule:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{dP}{dt} = {@duv@}\)</p>
<p>Simplify the expression:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{dP}{dt} = {@dsimolified@}\)</p>
<p>Using the trigonometric identity \( \displaystyle \sin^2(t) + \cos^2(t) = 1\), we can simplify further:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{dP}{dt} = {@ta@}\)</p>
<p>So, the derivative of the function \(\displaystyle P(t) = {@pt@}\) is:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle P'(t)={@ta@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
